          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Khovanov homology
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+invariant"} knot invariant
            that is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jones+polynomial"} Jones polynomial
          %p
            It is a member of the family of Khovanov-Rozansky link homology theories which categorify the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HOMFLY-PT+polynomial"} HOMFLY-PT polynomial
            and some of its one-variable specializations.
          %h2#interpretation_in_qft Interpretation in QFT
          %p
            Khovanov homology has long been expected to appear as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D4+TQFT"} D=4 TQFT
            in higher analogy of how the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jones+polynomial"} Jones polynomial
            arises as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observable"} quantum observable
            in 3-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D3+TQFT"} D=3 TQFT
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi K
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma \colon K \to K'
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knot+cobordism"} knot cobordism
            between two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/knots"} knots
            there is a natural morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Φ
                    %mi Σ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi 𝒦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Phi_\Sigma \colon \mathcal{K}(K) \to \mathcal{K}(K')
          %p between the Khovanov homologies associated to the two knots.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Witten11"} Witten 2011
            argued, following indications in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GukovSchwarzVafa04"} Gukov, Schwarz &amp; Vafa 2005
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4d+TQFT"} 4d TQFT
            is related to the worldvolume theory of the image in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIB+string+theory"} type IIB string theory
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D3-branes"} D3-branes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+intersection"} ending
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/NS5-branes"} NS5-branes
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
            of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 9
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^9 \times S^1
            with the circle transverse to both kinds of branes, under one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-duality"} S-duality
            and one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            operation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mn 3
                  %mo −
                  %mi NS
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mn 3
                  %mo −
                  %mi D
                  %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mn 4
                  %mo −
                  %mi D
                  %mn 6
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (D3-NS5)
                  \\stackrel{S}{\mapsto}
                  (D3-D5)
                  \\stackrel{T}{\mapsto}
                  (D4-D6)
                  \\,.
          %p
            To go from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jones+polynomial"} Jones polynomial
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Khovanov+homology"} Khovanov homology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            with the circle is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            (Witten index),
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Tr
                      %mi ℋ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi F
                    %msup
                      %mi e
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi β
                        %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Tr_{\mathcal{H}}(-1)^F e^{-\beta H}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            itself, rather than the index.
          %p
            (See
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/arxiv/pdf/1101/1101.3216v1.pdf#page=14"} Witten 2011, p. 14
          %p
            Earlier indication for this had come from the observation
            %a{:href => "#Witten92"} Witten92
            that Chern-Simons theory is the effective background theory for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A-model"} A-model
            2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TCFT"} TCFT
            (see
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/TCFT#ActionFunctionals"} TCFT – Worldsheet and effective background theories
            for details).
